        "axiom_use": "AC and Countable Choice are carried by the point-process/Palm supplier dependency closure; the local argument adds no DC.",
        "dependency_level": 5
      },
      {
        "id": "def-pair-correlation-and-ripleys-k-function",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Pair-correlation density and Ripley K-function",
        "deps": [
          "def-stationary-point-process-and-intensity",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "thm-stationary-point-process-intensity-measure-is-rho-lebesgue",
          "def-full-palm-distribution",
          "thm-refined-campbell-formula-and-palm-law-existence",
          "def-second-factorial-and-reduced-moment-measures",
          "thm-reduced-second-factorial-moment-palm-identity",
          "thm-radon-nikodym-density-exists-and-is-unique-up-to-almost-everywhere-equality",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "For a stationary locally square-integrable point process with intensity rho>0, a pair-correlation density is a measurable g>=0 satisfying alpha_red^(2)(du)=rho^2 g(u) du, when such a density exists. For r>=0 use the closed Euclidean ball B[0,r] and define K(r)=rho^{-1} E^0[(mu-delta_0)(B[0,r])]. This removes one distinguished occurrence and retains co-located atoms.",
        "strategy": "Definitions are conditional on absolute continuity for g and use the full Palm law for K; no density-existence claim is made.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "Günter Last and Mathew Penrose, Lectures on the Poisson Process (2017 author-hosted version)",
            "url": "https://stoch.math.kit.edu/img/Last/lastpenrose2017.pdf"
          },
          {
            "title": "Adrian Baddeley, Spatial Point Processes and their Applications (complete lecture notes)",
            "url": "https://leman.stat.vt.edu/VTCourses/BaddeleyPointProcesses.pdf"
          }
        ],
        "axiom_use": "AC and Countable Choice are carried by the point-process/Palm supplier dependency closure; the local argument adds no DC.",
        "dependency_level": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "alg-border-corrected-ripleys-k-estimator",
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Border-corrected estimator for Ripley K",
        "deps": [
          "def-pair-correlation-and-ripleys-k-function",
          "def-random-measure-and-locally-finite-point-process",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "Input a bounded Borel observation window W with 0<|W|<infinity, a finite observed counting measure xi on W, and r>=0. Put W_{-r}={x in W: the closed ball B[x,r] is contained in W}, N(A)=xi(A), and rhohat=N(W)/|W|. When rhohat>0 and N(W_{-r})>0, output [rhohat N(W_{-r})]^{-1} sum over ordered distinct occurrences x in xi restricted to W_{-r} and y in xi of 1{||y-x||<=r}; otherwise return not-defined. The anchor correction weight is 1 on W_{-r} and 0 on W\\W_{-r}. This procedure makes no unbiasedness, consistency, or asymptotic claim.",
        "strategy": "Definition-like finite-data procedure; the erosion guarantees that every radius-r neighborhood of an eligible anchor lies inside the observation window.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "Günter Last and Mathew Penrose, Lectures on the Poisson Process (2017 author-hosted version)",
            "url": "https://stoch.math.kit.edu/img/Last/lastpenrose2017.pdf"
          },
          {
            "title": "Adrian Baddeley, Spatial Point Processes and their Applications (complete lecture notes)",
            "url": "https://leman.stat.vt.edu/VTCourses/BaddeleyPointProcesses.pdf"
          }
        ],
        "axiom_use": "AC and Countable Choice are carried by the point-process supplier dependency closure; the local argument adds no DC.",
        "dependency_level": 7
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-homogeneous-poisson-ripleys-k-is-ball-volume",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Ripley K for a homogeneous Poisson process is Euclidean ball volume",
        "deps": [
          "def-pair-correlation-and-ripleys-k-function",
          "def-full-palm-distribution",
          "thm-refined-campbell-formula-and-palm-law-existence",
          "def-spatial-recentering-action",
