          "def-poisson-and-cox-point-process",
          "thm-poisson-mecke-identity",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "statement": "Assume AC and Countable Choice. If eta is a homogeneous Poisson point process on R^d with intensity rho dx, rho>0, then its full Palm law is the law of eta+delta_0 and K(r)=|B[0,r]| for every r>=0. Adding and deleting the distinguished atom means one occurrence, so no simplicity assumption is used.",
        "strategy": "Apply the deletion-form Mecke identity to identify the full Palm law, then remove its distinguished occurrence and use the intensity measure on the closed ball.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "Günter Last and Mathew Penrose, Lectures on the Poisson Process (2017 author-hosted version)",
